# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored on purpose — see below.
#
# VITE_RETURN_ADDRESS — where employees' progress reports are emailed.
#
# NOT COMMITTED, AND NOT A SECRET EITHER. This repo is public, so a real address
# here would be scraped; that is the only reason it lives in `.env`. The address
# is printed on the employee's plan sheet, so it is not confidential — treat it
# as "do not publish", not "do not disclose".
#
# Set it per machine, and in the Vercel project's environment variables when the
# client build is deployed. Unset is a real, handled state: the app tells the
# employee reporting is not set up yet rather than opening a mail client with no
# recipient (sendReturn -> 'unconfigured').
#
# BUILD-TIME, DELIBERATELY. Plan §4.2 has the return address travelling in the
# plan QR. It does not, and there is an argument in HANDOFF 2026-08-31 that it
# should not: as a build setting, changing it costs one redeploy and phones pick
# it up on next load. Carried in the QR, changing it means re-issuing every
# outstanding plan. The recognition key is the part that genuinely needs the
# contract decision.
VITE_RETURN_ADDRESS=

# VITE_CLIENT_BASE_URL — where an employee's phone lands when it scans a plan QR.
#
# Defaults to the admin's own origin, which is right while one build serves both
# halves. Set it once the client build is deployed separately, or every issued
# code points employees at the admin app.
#
# It is INSIDE the QR, so it spends capacity the plan needs: a long host can push
# an otherwise-fine plan over the limit. The issue page shows the byte count and
# the headroom for exactly that reason, and lets you edit the address per issue.
VITE_CLIENT_BASE_URL=
